00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:21.440
back down onto the bed. Noodle has become kind&nbsp;
of a guiding force for the daily mood of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:28.720
internet. On a bones day people are encouraged&nbsp;
to get out of bed and make the most of their day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:36.480
maybe take a risk or try something new, whereas&nbsp;
a no bones day might be the time to take it easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.160
and think a little bit about self-care. So what&nbsp;
does this have to do with quantum computing?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:49.040
Well almost nothing but I did notice that the&nbsp;
chance of a bones to a no bone day does seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:57.520
to be about 50 50. That's the same chance that a&nbsp;
qubit in an even superposition of states 0 and 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.560
would have of being measured in either&nbsp;
state. I recently found out that quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:07.280
computing is actually further ahead than&nbsp;
where I thought it was and in fact right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:14.560
now anyone can submit code to be run on a real&nbsp;
quantum computer so to figure out how to do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:22.800
I set out to ask a real quantum computer 'Bones&nbsp;
or no bones?' The answer might come from some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:29.680
point of deep intrinsic truth about our universe&nbsp;
or maybe it's just pretty close to truly random.

