00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.840
All right so it's important to&nbsp;
know what we're dealing with here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:45.840
In classical computing information is stored&nbsp;
using bits and these can either be zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.160
or one, they're binary and&nbsp;
they're like tiny little switches.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:03:00.400
Now bits all act independently of each other&nbsp;
and we can manipulate bits using logic gates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:03.840
There are quite a few different&nbsp;
kinds of logic gates but for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:12.800
one of them is the NOT gate. The NOT gate&nbsp;
can be drawn like this and it would turn a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:19.920
zero into a one or a one into a zero. Now when&nbsp;
we're dealing with quantum computing we're dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:28.640
with something called the qubit and these are just&nbsp;
quantum bits. We can represent qubits as an arrow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.160
pointing on the surface of a sphere.&nbsp;
Let's see if I can draw a sphere in here.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:45.840
I'll actually draw a couple.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:54.400
All right so if we have say an arrow pointing&nbsp;
on our sphere and if that arrow is pointing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:01.520
straight up well that qubit would be in the zero&nbsp;
state and if the arrow was pointing straight down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:08.880
then that qubit would be in the one state. But the&nbsp;
arrow can also point anywhere else on the sphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:17.040
Our arrow could point to the side like this and&nbsp;
this qubit is in a superposition of the zero and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:25.200
one states. Now when you measure a qubit you do&nbsp;
still only get out a zero or a one as the result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:31.120
and that's because measurement collapses the&nbsp;
superposition. If you were to keep measuring the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:38.960
same qubit again and again you would get the same&nbsp;
result. It is only before measurement takes place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:45.920
that a qubit is able to exist in multiple states&nbsp;
at once which gives it the ability to do things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:53.200
that classical bits can't. That means if you've&nbsp;
got a program that's working with qubits you'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:05:00.800
try to keep measurement to the last step in the&nbsp;
process. If we were to measure this superposition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:06.880
here we would find that because the arrow is&nbsp;
pointing halfway between the zero and one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:13.920
there would be a fifty percent chance of returning&nbsp;
a zero upon measurement and a fifty percent chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:20.320
of getting a one. If instead the arrow had been&nbsp;
pointing maybe more towards the zero that might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:26.080
have given an eighty percent chance of a zero,&nbsp;
twenty percent chance of one. And likewise down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.520
here this arrow might have given eighty percent&nbsp;
chance for one, twenty percent chance of a zero.

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:39.760
But physically how do you make a qubit? Well&nbsp;
there are actually several types. It's just any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:46.480
two-state quantum system such as the spin of an&nbsp;
electron or the polarization of a single photon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:54.560
IBM uses superconducting transmon cubits&nbsp;
which are a superconducting material&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:06:01.360
on a silicon chip arranged so that there is an&nbsp;
artificial atom with quantized energy levels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.840
and then kept extremely cold&nbsp;
in a ground state of zero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:12.880
Radiation can be applied to excite the qubit&nbsp;
to the one state or to enter a superposition.

