00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.320
my circuit it's actually fairly simple and&nbsp;
there's a diagram here of what's going on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:05.360
q is my one qubit and all qubits start initialized&nbsp;
at 0 which is their ground state but I want it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:13.440
to be in a 50/50 superposition so i've applied a&nbsp;
quantum logic gate that is the H symbol here. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:20.320
is the Hadamard gate. Now when it comes to quantum&nbsp;
logic gates there are some that are similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:28.160
their classical counterparts. For example there&nbsp;
is a quantum NOT gate and it's represented as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:35.440
X like the classical NOT gate that we looked&nbsp;
at. This one has the ability to turn a zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:42.800
into a one or a one into a zero. It essentially&nbsp;
flips the sign and we can even see how this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:52.480
represented physically with our sphere. If inside&nbsp;
our sphere we set up some axes so maybe a y there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.920 --> 00:08:03.520
an x, and up and down to be z, then you can&nbsp;
actually see where the quantum NOT gate gets its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:12.320
symbol the X from. It actually flips the arrow&nbsp;
over the x axis. So if we had a qubit that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:18.960
say a zero, meaning that the arrow was pointing&nbsp;
straight up, then if it was to be flipped across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:28.320
the x-axis it would come out looking like a one.&nbsp;
Likewise there are some quantum logic gates called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:35.360
y and z that flip it over those axes too. But&nbsp;
the H gate that we want to use in our circuit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:43.040
is kind of unique to the quantum world. After&nbsp;
passing through an H gate, a qubit that started as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:50.240
zero will have a fifty percent chance of being&nbsp;
zero and a fifty percent chance of being one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.040 --> 00:08:57.680
But the H gate isn't just a randomizer, for&nbsp;
example if you were to pass a qubit through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:04.640
two H gates one after the other it would basically&nbsp;
undo the effect and you would get back whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:10.160
value you started with and that's not behavior&nbsp;
you would expect from something that's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.160 --> 00:09:17.440
randomizing the results. Something else is going&nbsp;
on here. What the H gate is actually doing is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:27.040
applying a rotation around the line between&nbsp;
the x and the z axes so this line in here.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:37.680
Now you might be able to see that if an arrow&nbsp;
had been pointing straight up representing zero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:46.320
then flipping it over this line would put it here&nbsp;
halfway between zero and one meaning it has fifty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:51.760
percent chance of being measured as zero and&nbsp;
fifty percent chance of being measured as one.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:57.840
Then if you were here and you did another&nbsp;
rotation back around the same axis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:03.920
so you've applied your second H gate well you&nbsp;
would actually end up back where you started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:09.360
back at zero and so you can see&nbsp;
why two H gates do undo the effect.

